---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import ClassificationTable from '../components/ClassificationTable.astro';
import SimulatorLab from '../components/SimulatorLab.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="Probability Simulator: WinGo-Style Classifications" description="Run local 0-9 probability experiments with timed rounds, batch samples, frequencies, recorded replay, and CSV export. All assumptions are explained.">
  <div class="container"><Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: 'Simulator' }]} /></div>
  <header class="page-header container"><p class="eyebrow">The experiment desk</p><h1>Make chance<br /><em>a little more tangible.</em></h1><p>Run a local number experiment. See how a digit becomes a size and a set of colors, then compare the sample with the model.</p></header>
  <div class="container tool-layout"><SimulatorLab /><aside class="tool-sidebar"><span class="eyebrow">Before the first draw</span><h2>Know your model.</h2><ol class="numbered-list"><li><strong>Ten equally likely digits</strong><span>0 through 9, with a 10% chance for each.</span></li><li><strong>Independent model</strong><span>Previous outcomes do not change the theoretical chance of the next draw.</span></li><li><strong>Overlapping colors</strong><span>0 is Red + Violet. 5 is Green + Violet.</span></li></ol><a class="text-link" href="/number-probability/">Understand the probability <Icon /></a></aside></div>
  <section class="container section tool-explanation"><div class="prose"><h2>How outcomes are generated</h2><p>The sampler uses your browser’s <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Crypto/getRandomValues">random-value API</a>. It accepts unsigned 32-bit values below 4,294,967,290 and maps their remainder after division by ten to a digit. Rejecting the final six possible values avoids modulo bias under a uniform word source.</p><p>This implements a model of independent draws. A browser generator is not proof of mathematical independence or certified external fairness. No operator is contacted and no prediction is made.</p><h2>Run, pause, and inspect</h2><p>Batch samples contain 1–10,000 draws and run in bounded chunks so you can cancel. Timed experiments use five-second teaching rounds, with 1–100 rounds per run. The timer changes the pace, not the model. Timed progression pauses while the page is hidden and does not invent a background catch-up history.</p><p>Replay restores the last completed recorded dataset without drawing again. Export downloads that completed dataset as CSV with the model, mode, sample size, digits, and classifications. Cancelling does not record the partial run. If a previous completed dataset exists, replay/export still refer to that last completed run. Reset clears both the visible run and the recorded dataset; reload clears local run history.</p><h2>Reading the classifications</h2><p>The table is the selected example ruleset. A digit has one size, but 0 and 5 have two color memberships. Other versions may use different rules.</p><ClassificationTable /><h2>Observed is different from expected</h2><p>In 20 draws, the expected count for a particular digit is 2. It need not appear exactly twice. Counts use the entire run, even though the recent-history list shows only the latest 20 results. The <a href="/historical-analysis/">historical-analysis guide</a> explains sampling variation.</p><div class="takeaway"><p>Local educational simulation only. No live results, external predictions, accounts, or financial transactions.</p></div></div></section>
</BaseLayout>
